  { q: 'Why did God use ravens to feed Elijah?', a: 'The text does not give a reason for the choice of ravens specifically. Rabbinic tradition and Christian commentators have offered various interpretations: ravens are unclean birds (Leviticus 11:15) used by God in a miraculous act to show that he provides even through unexpected means; or the raven was chosen because it is a natural scavenger associated with remote wilderness, fitting the landscape of Elijah\'s hiding. The choice of ravens adds to the miraculous character of the narrative — an impure bird bringing pure food to God\'s prophet.' },
  { q: 'Is the story of ravens feeding Elijah historical?', a: 'The story is presented in the Hebrew Bible as a historical event. It is not independently corroborated by non-biblical sources, and scholarly opinion on the historicity of the Elijah narratives varies widely. For religious traditions that accept the Bible as sacred history, the feeding is a literal miracle. For historians and biblical scholars, the narrative is studied as religious literature from the 9th century BCE or later, with possible oral tradition behind it.' },
];
